/**
@page tutorial_backends Multi-Backend Tutorial

@tableofcontents

@c database_system targets four backends — SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL, and
MongoDB — through a single @c database_backend interface plus a runtime
@c backend_registry. Application code stays the same; only the connection
string and the @c set_mode() argument change.

This tutorial covers backend-specific features, the difference between
direct and proxy connection modes, and a portable migration path between
backends.

@section be_matrix Backend Capability Matrix

| Feature | SQLite | PostgreSQL | MySQL | MongoDB |
|---------|--------|------------|-------|---------|
| ACID transactions | yes | yes | yes (InnoDB) | per-document |
| Concurrent writers | single | many | many | many |
| SQL dialect | SQLite | ANSI + extensions | MySQL | n/a (BSON) |
| Auto-increment | @c INTEGER @c PRIMARY @c KEY @c AUTOINCREMENT | @c SERIAL / @c IDENTITY | @c AUTO_INCREMENT | @c _id |
| Server-side pooling | n/a (file) | yes | yes | yes |
| Recommended use | embedded, tests, edge | OLTP, analytics, JSON | OLTP, web | document store |

@section be_select Selecting a Backend

The same client code drives any backend; only the @c database_types value
and the connection string change.

@code{.cpp}
#include "database/database_manager.h"
#include "database/core/database_context.h"

using namespace database;

std::shared_ptr<database_manager> open_db(database_types type, std::string conn)
{
    auto ctx = std::make_shared<database_context>();
    auto mgr = std::make_shared<database_manager>(ctx);

    if (!mgr->set_mode(type))
        throw std::runtime_error("Backend not compiled in");

    auto r = mgr->connect_result(conn);
    if (!r.is_ok())
        throw std::runtime_error(r.error().message);

    return mgr;
}

// SQLite (embedded, file-backed)
auto sqlite_db = open_db(database_types::sqlite, "file:app.sqlite3");

// PostgreSQL (libpqxx connection string)
auto pg_db = open_db(database_types::postgres,
    "host=localhost port=5432 dbname=app user=app password=secret");

// MySQL
auto mysql_db = open_db(database_types::mysql,
    "host=localhost;port=3306;dbname=app;user=app;password=secret");

// MongoDB
auto mongo_db = open_db(database_types::mongodb,
    "mongodb://localhost:27017/app");
@endcode

@warning Some backends are gated behind CMake options. Build with
@c -DUSE_POSTGRESQL=ON, @c -DUSE_SQLITE=ON, @c -DUSE_MONGODB=ON, or
@c -DUSE_REDIS=ON depending on which adapters you need.

@section be_features Backend-Specific Features

@subsection be_sqlite SQLite

Best for embedded apps, unit tests, and edge devices. The "connection
string" is just the file path (or @c :memory: for an in-memory database).

@code{.cpp}
auto sqlite_db = open_db(database_types::sqlite, ":memory:");

sqlite_db->execute_query_result("PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL");
sqlite_db->execute_query_result("PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON");
@endcode

Use SQLite for tests so they do not need an external server. Pair with
@c PRAGMA @c journal_mode=WAL for higher concurrency.

@subsection be_pg PostgreSQL

The reference relational backend, built on @c libpqxx. Supports schemas,
@c JSONB, partial indexes, @c RETURNING clauses, and prepared statements.

@code{.cpp}
pg_db->execute_query_result(
    "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS audit ("
    "  id    BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,"
    "  data  JSONB     NOT NULL,"
    "  ts    TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT now()"
    ")");

auto inserted = pg_db->select_query_result(
    "INSERT INTO audit (data) VALUES ('{\"k\":1}'::jsonb) RETURNING id, ts");
@endcode

@subsection be_mysql MySQL

Use InnoDB tables for transactional safety. The backend honors
backtick-quoted identifiers and the standard @c AUTO_INCREMENT semantics.

@code{.cpp}
mysql_db->create_query_result(
    "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS sessions ("
    "  id        BIGINT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,"
    "  user_id   BIGINT NOT NULL,"
    "  token     VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL UNIQUE,"
    "  expires   DATETIME NOT NULL,"
    "  INDEX idx_user (user_id)"
    ") ENGINE=InnoDB");
@endcode

@subsection be_mongo MongoDB

The MongoDB adapter exposes the same @c execute / @c select API but maps
each statement to a BSON command. Use it when your data is naturally
document-shaped or schemaless.

@code{.cpp}
mongo_db->execute_query_result(
    R"({ "insert": "events",
         "documents": [ { "type":"login", "ts":1700000000 } ] })");

auto rows = mongo_db->select_query_result(
    R"({ "find": "events", "filter": { "type":"login" } })");
@endcode

@note MongoDB and Redis are currently experimental. They are disabled by
default and have limited test coverage. Treat them as preview backends.

@section be_modes Connection Modes: Direct vs Proxy

@c database_system supports two conceptual connection modes.

| Mode | When to use | Notes |
|------|-------------|-------|
| **Direct mode** (default) | Process talks straight to the database driver. | Lowest latency, simplest deployment, fewest moving parts. |
| **Proxy mode** | Many client processes share a centralised pool. | Reduces driver-side connection counts, enables centralised auth and metrics. |

In direct mode the @c database_manager owns its driver handle and any
connection pooling happens at the driver layer. Choose this when:

- The process is long-lived (servers, daemons)
- You only need a single application sharing the database
- You want the smallest deployment

@code{.cpp}
auto ctx = std::make_shared<database_context>();
auto mgr = std::make_shared<database_manager>(ctx);
mgr->set_mode(database_types::postgres);
mgr->connect_result("host=db.internal port=5432 dbname=app user=svc password=...");
// Direct mode: this process owns the libpqxx connection
@endcode

Proxy mode is the planned alternative where many clients share an
external @c database_server pool. The client API is identical — the
runtime simply marshals requests to the proxy instead of the driver.

@warning Proxy mode is currently a stub. Local connection pooling was
removed in Phase 4.3 in anticipation of the proxy implementation. Until
then, rely on direct mode plus driver-level pooling.

@section be_migration Migrating Between Backends

Because every backend implements the same @c database_backend interface,
moving an application from SQLite to PostgreSQL (or any other pair) only
requires three changes: the @c database_types argument, the connection
string, and any backend-specific DDL.

A typical migration follows this pattern:

@code{.cpp}
// 1. Open both connections
auto src = open_db(database_types::sqlite, "file:legacy.sqlite3");
auto dst = open_db(database_types::postgres,
    "host=localhost port=5432 dbname=app user=app password=secret");

// 2. Re-create the schema on the destination using portable DDL
dst->create_query_result(R"(
    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS users (
        id    BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
        name  TEXT NOT NULL,
        email TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE
    )
)");

// 3. Stream rows from source to destination inside a transaction
auto rows = src->select_query_result("SELECT id, name, email FROM users");
if (rows.is_ok())
{
    dst->begin_transaction();
    for (const auto& row : rows.value())
    {
        std::string name, email;
        for (const auto& [col, val] : row)
        {
            if (col == "name")  name  = std::get<std::string>(val);
            if (col == "email") email = std::get<std::string>(val);
        }

        dst->execute_query_result(
            "INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES ('" + name + "','" + email + "')");
    }
    dst->commit_transaction();
}
@endcode

@note Production migrations should use parameterised statements or the
query builder to avoid string-concatenation injection risks. See
@ref query_builder_usage.cpp for parameterised patterns.

@section be_next Next Steps

- @ref tutorial_quickstart — CRUD primer
- @ref tutorial_orm — Map C++ classes to portable schemas
- @ref faq — Backend selection and pooling questions
- @ref troubleshooting — Connection failures and dialect mismatches
- @ref basic_connection.cpp — Runnable connection sample

*/
